---
import { getImage } from "astro:assets"
import reportForm from "../../../public/assets/pivo-cs-current.jpg"
import CaseStudyDemo from "../../components/CaseStudyDemo.astro"
import CaseStudyLayout from "../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro"

const previews = await Promise.all(
  [480, 800, reportForm.width].map(width => getImage({ src: reportForm, width, format: "webp" })),
)
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  path="/work/pivo-cs-qa/"
  title="Pivo CS / QA support report triage with React — Jérôme Schaeffer"
  description="How Jérôme Schaeffer built Pivo’s React intranet for customer support and QA. Follow a sample report from the queue to a status change and an investigation note."
  heading="Triaging support reports at Pivo"
  image={previews[2].src}
  imageWidth={reportForm.width}
  imageHeight={reportForm.height}
  srcset={previews.map(image => `${image.src} ${image.attributes.width}w`).join(", ")}
  sizes="(min-width: 1174px) 1080px, (max-width: 500px) calc(100vw - 40px), 92vw"
  imageAlt="Pivo’s empty New report form, with a Zendesk ticket import above the issue title and description."
>
  <Fragment slot="lead">
    “The preview froze” gives support a symptom. QA also needs to know which device was in use and what the rider
    did just before the freeze. At 3i, I independently designed and built the Pivo CS / QA app, a React intranet
    that keeps a customer report and the team’s investigation together.
  </Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="caption">
    The New report screen in the internal app. Support can start from a Zendesk ticket number or URL.
  </Fragment>

  <section class="chapter" aria-labelledby="intake">
    <h2 id="intake">What carries over from a support ticket?</h2>
    <div>
      <p>
        Importing a Zendesk ticket brings its subject, description, and attachments into the report. Support can
        then add device and app details and assign the issue. QA can trace the investigation through to its root
        cause and release.
      </p>
      <p>
        The dashboard groups work by status. Each filter shows how many reports it contains. Opening a preview
        reveals the issue summary and device, so someone scanning the queue can decide which report to investigate.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="chapter" aria-labelledby="handoff">
    <h2 id="handoff">What does the next investigator need?</h2>
    <div>
      <p>
        In one fictional report, a video preview freezes on the last frame when an Android tablet rotates during
        playback. The preview identifies the device. The full report gives QA a place to record which steps reproduced
        the freeze and what they plan to check next.
      </p>
      <p>
        Changing the status moves the report into the corresponding queue. An internal note stays with the report,
        so the next person who opens it can pick up the investigation where their colleague left off.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="demo-section" aria-labelledby="demo-title">
    <h2 id="demo-title">Triage a sample report</h2>
    <p>
      Choose Unresolved and open the frozen-preview report. In the full report, set its status to Pending and add
      a note. Return to the queue and find it under Pending. The four reports are fictional, and Restart restores
      their original statuses and clears your notes.
    </p>
    <CaseStudyDemo demo="cs" label="Open triage demo" fallback="Enable JavaScript to try the support report queue." />
  </section>
</CaseStudyLayout>
